/*
  core/usability_properties.spthy -- the USABILITY-SECURITY PROPERTY LIBRARY (ROADMAP #3).

  Because the human layer now speaks ONE uniform vocabulary (every stressor emits SetMask + an onset
  event; every mask emits RespMask + its outcome facts; targeting is StressOn -> !StressEnable), some
  usability-security properties are CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC and provable for ANY profile that includes this
  file. A designer gets them for free:

    UP_degradation_is_attributable  -- no human deviates spontaneously: every degraded response is
                                       preceded by a stressor that set that mask. (No "the human just
                                       did the wrong thing" -- there is always a named cause.)
    UP_degradation_requires_targeting -- a stressor degrades only a party that was ENABLED/targeted
                                       (StressOn), i.e. the analysis cannot conjure a stressor from
                                       nowhere; degradations are accountable to a modelled condition.

  Proven cross-ceremony (included in P3 worst-case and S0 secure_email). The other canonical
  properties depend on the ceremony's own security goal, so they ship as TEMPLATES below: copy the
  form, fill the <placeholders> with your ceremony's facts. Outcome vocabulary (uniform): unsafe =
  Slip / Mistake / AutoApprove / LeakPw / MisdeliveredPw ; safe-fail = Timeout / Abort ; onsets =
  Load / TimePressure / Abstraction / Habituate / AlertVolume / SecurityAnxiety / Distract /
  RepeatedFailure ; degradation = SetMask(p,m) / RespMask(p,m), m != 'Attentive'.
*/

// ===== generic, includable (hold for any profile) =====

// Attribution backbone: a degraded RESPONSE only occurs after a stressor set that mask for that party.
lemma UP_degradation_is_attributable:
  all-traces
  "All p m #i. RespMask(p, m)@i & not(m = 'Attentive')
     ==> (Ex #s. SetMask(p, m)@s & #s < #i)"

// Targeting soundness: a degrading SetMask only fires for a party that was stress-enabled (StressOn).
lemma UP_degradation_requires_targeting:
  all-traces
  "All p m #i. SetMask(p, m)@i & not(m = 'Attentive')
     ==> (Ex #s. StressOn(p)@s & #s < #i)"

/*
  ===== TEMPLATES (copy into your experiments/<profile>.spthy and fill <...>) =====

  T1 -- USABILITY-ROBUSTNESS (headline): the security goal holds on every trace with no unsafe outcome.
        all-traces
        "All <g-vars> #g. <SecurityGoal>(...)@g
           & not (Ex ... Mistake(...))  & not (Ex ... LeakPw(...))  & not (Ex ... <other unsafe>)
         ==> not (<Breach>(...))"
        Filled example: S0_attentive_keeps_secret (Secret(m) & no Mistake/LeakPw/MisdeliveredPw ==> not K(m)).

  T2 -- OUTCOME-REQUIRES-STRESSOR (per-stressor safety / mitigatability anchor): a bad outcome implies
        its driving stressor fired first -- so removing/mitigating that stressor removes the outcome.
        all-traces
        "All p x #o. <BadOutcome>(p, x)@o ==> (Ex #s. <Onset>(p)@s & #s < #o)"
        Filled examples: P2_mistake_requires_abstraction (Mistake ==> Abstraction);
                         P4_escalation_requires_failure (RepeatedFailure ==> prior Slip).

  T3 -- BREACH ATTRIBUTION: any adversary win traces to ONE named unsafe human outcome (a single weak
        point the RFC must address).
        all-traces
        "All m #s #k. <Secret>(m)@s & <AdvWins>(m)@k
           ==> (Ex ... Mistake(...)) | (Ex ... LeakPw(...)) | (Ex ... <other unsafe>)"
        Filled example: S0_message_secrecy_attribution.

  T4 -- INTERFACE / MITIGATION LEVER (the RFC requirement, a PAIR): show the bad outcome is reachable
        under the baseline interface/config but unreachable under the hardened one.
        baseline:  exists-trace "Ex p x #o. <BadOutcome>(p,x)@o"          (e.g. P2_mistake_reachable)
        hardened:  all-traces  "not (Ex p x #o. <BadOutcome>(p,x)@o)"     (e.g. P7_no_mistake)
        The pair => "implementations MUST use the hardened interface/mitigation."
*/
